<h1>How to scan documents with your phone (no scanner app needed)</h1>
<p>Your phone camera plus a free browser tool is all you need for clean, readable scans — for contracts, receipts, IDs, forms, and homework.</p>
<div class="article-meta">
<span>Jan 18, 2026</span>
<span>6 min read</span>
<span>By the editors</span>
</div>
</div>

<div class="content">
<h2>Why phone scans beat a real scanner</h2>
<p>For 95% of everyday documents — receipts, forms, signed pages, ID copies, invoices — a well-shot phone photo is faster, sharper, and easier to share than a dedicated scanner. Most modern phones have 12–50 megapixel cameras that resolve text fine at arm's length. The real problem is almost never the camera; it's <strong>how the photo is cropped, lit, and compressed</strong>.</p>
<p>That's a five-minute fix. This guide walks through it step by step, using only free browser tools. No app installs, no accounts, no uploads to a random server.</p>

<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul>
<li>A phone with a camera (any recent model is fine).</li>
<li>A flat surface with soft, even light. A window near midday is ideal.</li>
<li>A browser. That's it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 1 — Shoot it right</h2>
<p>Bad source photos can't be rescued later. Four things matter:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flat, straight angle.</strong> Hold the phone parallel to the page, not tilted. Tilt creates trapezoid distortion that cropping can't fix.</li>
<li><strong>Even lighting.</strong> Avoid overhead lights that cast half the page into shadow. Diffuse daylight from a window is best.</li>
<li><strong>Fill the frame.</strong> Get close enough that the document occupies most of the image — more pixels on the text means better OCR later.</li>
<li><strong>Tap to focus on the text.</strong> Phones default to focusing on the nearest face or the brightest area. Tap directly on the page to force focus there.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're using the built-in camera on this site, the 2× or 5× zoom pills help you get closer without moving the phone — good for avoiding shadow across the page.</p>

<h2>Step 2 — Crop tight</h2>
<p>After uploading, open the <strong>Crop</strong> tool. Drag the handles to the four corners of the document and leave about 2–3 mm of margin on each side — no more. Everything outside the page is wasted pixels and noise the enhancer will try to correct.</p>
<p>If the page has a slight tilt, don't try to fix it with crop — Cropper.js keeps the frame rectangular. Instead, retake the shot with the phone flatter, or accept the tilt if it's under 2°.</p>
<p>Click <strong>Apply Crop</strong>.</p>

<h2>Step 3 — Enhance for readability</h2>
<p>This is the step most people skip. It's also the one that makes the biggest difference. Open the <strong>Enhance</strong> tool and adjust:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contrast:</strong> push to 130–150%. This is the single most important slider for text. It separates black ink from grey paper.</li>
<li><strong>Brightness:</strong> raise to 110–120% if the page looks grey or the light was dim. Lower to 90% if it's blown out.</li>
<li><strong>Saturation:</strong> drop to 0% if you want a clean black-and-white scan. This is what real scanner apps do — it removes the yellowish cast from indoor lighting.</li>
<li><strong>Clarity:</strong> 30–50% sharpens text edges. Above 60% it starts to introduce halos around letters.</li>
</ul>
<p>Click <strong>Apply to Image</strong>. You can also apply a <strong>B&amp;W</strong> filter from the Filters tool for a one-click monochrome look.</p>

<h2>Step 4 — Compress for email and uploads</h2>
<p>Scanned documents don't need to be 5 MB. Most upload forms cap at 200 KB – 2 MB, and emails choke above 10 MB. Open the <strong>Compress</strong> tool and:</p>
<ul>
<li>Set the format to <strong>JPEG</strong> (not PNG — PNG is 3–5× larger for no benefit here).</li>
<li>Drop quality to <strong>75–85%</strong>. For text documents this is visually identical to 100%.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Compress &amp; Download</strong>. Check the file size shown in the tool panel. If it's still too big, retry at 65%.</li>
</ul>
<p>A typical A4 document photographed at 3000 × 4000 px and compressed to JPEG at 80% lands around 300–600 KB. If you need under 200 KB, resize to 1200 px wide first, then compress — the text is still perfectly readable.</p>

<h2>Step 5 — Extract the text (optional)</h2>
<p>If you need the text editable or searchable — for copy-pasting a contract, digitising a receipt, or making a homework answer sheet — open the <strong>Read Text (OCR)</strong> tool, pick the language, and click Extract. Tesseract handles clean, high-contrast scans well; 95–99% character accuracy is realistic for printed text.</p>
<p>If OCR is your goal, prioritise Steps 1–3 (sharp, straight, high-contrast) over compression. Compressing too aggressively before OCR introduces blur that drops accuracy noticeably.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes that ruin phone scans</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shadow across the page.</strong> The most common failure. Shoot perpendicular to a window, not under a ceiling light.</li>
<li><strong>Tilted phone.</strong> Creates trapezoid distortion. If the page looks narrower at the top than the bottom, retake the shot.</li>
<li><strong>Cropping too wide.</strong> Leaving the desk, the coffee cup, and half a hand in the frame confuses the enhancer and bloats the file.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping the contrast step.</strong> Grey, flat scans look unprofessional and trip up OCR.</li>
<li><strong>Exporting to PNG.</strong> Unless the document has photographs that need maximum fidelity, JPEG is always the right format.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to use a real scanner</h2>
<p>Phone scans are excellent for single-page documents. But there are cases where a dedicated scanner still wins:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Multi-page documents</strong> — a scanner with a feeder processes 30 pages in a minute; you'd take 30 minutes by hand.</li>
<li><strong>Legal or archival scans</strong> where 600 DPI and colour fidelity are required.</li>
<li><strong>Bound books</strong> — a flatbed scanner with a book edge handles spine curvature; a phone photo can't.</li>
</ul>
<p>For everything else — the receipt you need for expenses, the signed contract to email back, the ID copy for a form — the five-step workflow above will produce a cleaner, smaller file than any app on your phone.</p>

